PLACES=[
N('roswell-mill-ruins','Roswell Mill ruins','Roswell','ga','industry','ruins',34.01336,-84.35669,'area','public','walk','burned','1836–1975',
  'Roswell King, who had managed Pierce Butler\'s coastal plantations, began a cotton mill on Vickery Creek in 1836, building it with the labour of people he enslaved; a second mill followed in 1853 and another in 1882, and the group became the largest cotton mill in north Georgia, making wool and flour as well. In 1864 Union troops burned the mills and sent the mill workers, most of them women, north as prisoners. Rebuilt, the mill ran until 1975.',
  'Remnants of several mill buildings along Vickery Creek, and a covered bridge over the creek that has been rebuilt. The 1882 mill building still stands up the hill and is now offices. The King family\'s two brick rows of workers\' housing, "The Bricks", stand nearby.',
  'The mill whose workers Sherman shipped north.',
  'The ruins are in Old Mill Park, a City of Roswell park off Sloan Street, and the trails across the creek are the Vickery Creek unit of the Chattahoochee River National Recreation Area (National Park Service), open from dawn to dusk year round by the Park Service\'s hours page. Parking for the park itself was not checked; a Park Service lot is on Riverside Road at the south end of the unit.',
  [W('Roswell Mill'),['NPS: Chattahoochee River hours','https://www.nps.gov/chat/planyourvisit/hours.htm'],
   ['NPS: Ivy Mill ruins, Vickery Creek unit','https://www.nps.gov/places/ivy-mill-ruins-place.htm']],
  brick='?',hours='Dawn to dusk'),

N('barnsley-gardens-ruins','Barnsley Gardens manor house ruins (Woodlands)','Adairsville','ga','inst','ruins',34.29853,-84.98762,'area','open','walk','burned','late 1840s–1906',
  'Godfrey Barnsley, a cotton merchant from Liverpool, began an Italianate villa called Woodlands in the late 1840s on land taken from the Cherokee Nation, built for his wife Julia, who died before it was finished. Barnsley lost his fortune in the Civil War, and the house was burned during the war, Wikipedia says. His family lived on until a tornado tore off the main roof in 1906; they moved into the kitchen wing and the house fell to ruin. In 1988 Prince Hubertus Fugger bought the estate, stabilized the ruins and restored the gardens, and it is now Barnsley Resort.',
  'The shell of the manor house, a boxwood parterre garden in front of it whose box hedges date from the early 1840s, and a small museum of family photographs and artifacts. Barnsley\'s grave was moved to the gardens by the ruins.',
  'A Liverpool merchant\'s Italian villa, roofless in the north Georgia hills.',
  'Barnsley Resort (private). Free for overnight guests and members; day passes are sold to other visitors, by the resort\'s own page, which gives no opening times. The house ruins are used for private events.',
  [W('Barnsley Gardens'),['Barnsley Resort: Manor House Ruins','https://www.barnsleyresort.com/recreation/manor-house-ruins/']],
  brick='?',hours='?'),

N('springer-opera-house','Springer Opera House','Columbus','ga','civic','standing',32.46513,-84.99134,'exact','open','drive','','1871',
  'Francis Joseph Springer, a grocer from Alsace, built this theatre on the site of his store at Tenth Street and First Avenue; it opened on February 21, 1871. Edwin Booth, Lillie Langtry, Oscar Wilde and John Philip Sousa played here. After decades of decline a local group saved it, restored the main hall and reopened it in 1965, and a 1998 project reclaimed the second and third floors that had stood in ruins. It is a National Historic Landmark and the State Theatre of Georgia.',
  'A working theatre with Victorian decor, balconies and ceiling frescoes; the painting round the proscenium arch was restored in 1998. Inside on guided tours or at a show.',
  'Where Edwin Booth and Oscar Wilde played, still staging shows.',
  'Springer Opera House (nonprofit), 103 Tenth Street, downtown Columbus. Historical tours are booked ahead by email or telephone, $10 a person with a five-person minimum, by the theatre\'s tours page; ghost tours on set dates.',
  [W('Springer Opera House'),['Springer: building tours','https://www.springeroperahouse.org/building-tours']],
  brick='?',hours='Shows on the theatre\'s calendar; historical tours booked ahead'),

N('windsor-hotel-americus','Windsor Hotel','Americus','ga','hotel','standing',32.07235,-84.23354,'site','open','drive','hotels','1892',
  'A five-storey Queen Anne hotel of a hundred rooms round a three-storey atrium, built in 1892 to draw winter visitors from the northeast and designed by the Swedish-born Atlanta architect Gottfried Leonard Norrman. Vice-President Thomas Marshall spoke from its balcony in 1917 and Franklin Roosevelt in its dining room in 1928. It closed in the early 1970s and later reopened with 53 rooms.',
  'The towered Queen Anne front on West Lamar Street, and the three-storey atrium inside. Interior not checked beyond that.',
  'A Victorian winter-resort hotel still taking guests.',
  'A working hotel, 125 West Lamar Street, in the Americus Historic District. Opening of the public rooms to non-guests not checked.',
  [W('Windsor Hotel (Americus, Georgia)')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),

N('plains-high-school-depot','Plains High School and Plains Depot','Plains','ga','civic','standing',32.03658,-84.39256,'exact','open','drive','stations','1921',
  'Plains High School, opened in 1921 and closed in 1979, taught both Jimmy and Rosalynn Carter; it is now the museum and visitor center of the Jimmy Carter National Historical Park, with a classroom, principal\'s office and auditorium restored in 1996 to their look in Carter\'s day. A short walk away the town\'s railroad depot, used from 1888 to 1951, was Carter\'s campaign headquarters in 1976 and is now a museum of that campaign.',
  'The school\'s restored classroom, office and auditorium, and the small depot on West Main Street. The park also holds Carter\'s boyhood farm, whose house is restored to before 1938; the Carter residence is closed, but the gravesite and memorial garden beside it are open.',
  'The school the Carters went to and the depot that ran a winning campaign.',
  'Jimmy Carter National Historical Park (National Park Service), 300 North Bond Street. Admission free. Hours are the Park Service\'s, for the visitor information desk at the school.',
  [W('Jimmy Carter National Historical Park'),['NPS: Jimmy Carter NHP hours','https://www.nps.gov/jica/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  brick='yes',hours='Visitor desk 9:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. daily; closed January 1, Thanksgiving and December 25'),

N('bridge-house-albany','Bridge House (Albany Welcome Center)','Albany','ga','landmark','standing',31.57778,-84.14889,'exact','open','drive','','1858',
  'Nelson Tift hired Horace King, an engineer born into slavery and freed by the Alabama legislature, to build a covered bridge over the Flint River in 1858, and King\'s design included this house as the gateway to the bridge and the city. Its cellars packed meat for Confederate soldiers in the Civil War. The county built a new bridge south of it after 1887; the house was restored and reopened as the Albany Welcome Center in 2008.',
  'The restored house at 112 North Front Street by the Flint River. The bridge is gone. Interior not checked.',
  'The gateway house a formerly enslaved bridge builder designed.',
  'Albany Welcome Center (city). Opening times not checked.',
  [W('Bridge House (Albany, Georgia)')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),

N('lapham-patterson-house','Lapham-Patterson House','Thomasville','ga','landmark','standing',30.84562,-83.98296,'exact','open','drive','','1884–1885',
  'A winter cottage built in 1884–85 for C. W. Lapham, a Chicago shoe merchant who came to Thomasville, then a resort town, for its mild climate and pine air. It had its own gas lighting, hot and cold running water, indoor plumbing and closets. The Laphams sold it in 1894 and it was sold again in 1905 to James G. Patterson, whose family kept it until 1970. It is a National Historic Landmark and a state historic site.',
  'Fishscale shingles, oriental-style porch ornament, inlaid longleaf pine floors, and a double-flue chimney with a stairway that passes through it, inside on guided tours.',
  'A Victorian winter cottage with a staircase through its chimney.',
  'Lapham-Patterson House State Historic Site (Georgia Department of Natural Resources, managed by the Thomasville History Center), 626 North Dawson Street. Adults $15, children $10. Leashed pets on the grounds, not in buildings. Hours from the state park page.',
  [['Georgia State Parks: Lapham-Patterson House',GSP+'LaphamPattersonHouse']],
  brick='?',hours='Guided tours Friday 1, 2 and 3 p.m.; Saturday 10 and 11 a.m., 1, 2 and 3 p.m.'),

N('pebble-hill-plantation','Pebble Hill Plantation','Thomasville','ga','inst','standing',30.78022,-84.06386,'exact','open','drive','','1820s; house 1936',
  'Thomas Jefferson Johnson built the first house here in the 1820s, and the people the family enslaved grew cotton, tobacco and rice. Howard Melville Hanna bought the place in 1896, and his daughter Kate made it a hunting estate. The main house burned in 1934 and Abram Garfield designed the present one, finished in 1936. Kate\'s daughter, Elizabeth "Pansy" Ireland, left a foundation that opens it to the public.',
  'The 1936 main house, shown on guided tours, and the grounds. Interior details not checked.',
  'A quail-hunting estate of the Thomasville winter colony.',
  'Pebble Hill Foundation (private, open to visitors), off US 319 south of Thomasville. Admission charged; house tours hold at most eight people. Leashed dogs welcome on the grounds. Hours from the plantation\'s own page.',
  [W('Pebble Hill Plantation'),['Pebble Hill: tours and admission','https://pebblehill.com/tours-admission']],
  brick='?',hours='Grounds Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday noon to 5; house tours Tuesday to Saturday 10:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.'),

N('chesser-island-homestead','Chesser Island Homestead','Okefenokee National Wildlife Refuge (Folkston)','ga','landmark','standing',30.71541,-82.15544,'exact','public','walk','','1927',
  'W. T. Chesser settled this island on the eastern edge of the Okefenokee Swamp in the late 1850s, and his family lived on what they could hunt, catch and grow, with sugar cane, tobacco and turpentine for cash. His grandson Tom Chesser and Tom\'s wife Iva built the present house of yellow pine in 1927, for about $200 by family account; it began with four rooms and an indoor kitchen. The refuge keeps it as it was.',
  'The pine house and its outbuildings, and a yard swept bare of all plants, as was done to keep fire off and snakes in sight. A 0.7-mile loop trail goes round the homestead, and the Deer Stand Trail goes on to the Chesser Island Boardwalk.',
  'A swamper family\'s pine homestead on an island in the Okefenokee.',
  'Okefenokee National Wildlife Refuge (US Fish and Wildlife Service), on Swamp Island Drive from the refuge\'s east entrance near Folkston. Park at stop 11 of the drive and walk the trail to the homestead; a brochure is at the start of the trail. Refuge hours and the entrance fee were not checked.',
  [W('Chesser Island'),['USFWS: Chesser Island Homestead','https://www.fws.gov/locations/okefenokee-national-wildlife-refuge/chesser-island-homestead']],
  th=[30.71391,-82.15588],thn='Lot on Chesser Island Road (stop 11)',brick='no',hours='?'),

N('georgia-state-railroad-museum','Georgia State Railroad Museum (Central of Georgia shops)','Savannah','ga','rail','standing',32.07571,-81.10126,'site','open','walk','stations','1853',
  'The Central of Georgia Railway built its repair shops and terminal here on Louisville Road beginning in the 1850s, before the Civil War; it is called the most complete antebellum railroad complex in the United States and is a National Historic Landmark. The Coastal Heritage Society runs it as a railroad and industry museum with the City of Savannah.',
  'A partial roundhouse with a working turntable, part of the machine shop, the tender frame shop, blacksmith shop, boiler house, storehouse and print shop, lumber and planing sheds, and coach and paint shops. The Central of Georgia depot and trainshed stand across the street.',
  'America\'s most complete pre-Civil War railroad shops, turntable still turning.',
  'Georgia State Railroad Museum (Coastal Heritage Society), 655 Louisville Road. Admission charged. Hours not checked: the society\'s page would not open when this was written.',
  [W('Georgia State Railroad Museum')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),

N('jarrell-plantation','Jarrell Plantation','Juliette','ga','landmark','standing',33.05269,-83.72342,'site','open','walk','','1847–1974',
  'John Fitz Jarrell built a heart pine house here in 1847 and farmed cotton with the forced labour of enslaved people, 39 to 42 of them by the early 1860s, Wikipedia and the state giving different counts. The farm lived through typhoid, Sherman\'s march and emancipation. In 1895 his son Dick built a house for his twelve children and ran a sawmill, cotton gin, gristmill, shingle mill, planer, cane press and syrup evaporator by steam. The family farmed it for over 140 years and gave the buildings to the state in 1974.',
  'The 1847 and 1895 houses with furniture the family made, and the steam-powered mill buildings and outbuildings, on a self-guided tour; a museum, film and history trail.',
  'One family\'s farm, steam mills and all, from slavery to the 1970s.',
  'Jarrell Plantation State Historic Site (Georgia Department of Natural Resources), Jones County. Adults $7, seniors $5.25, youth $4.50. Leashed pets on trails, not in buildings. Hours from the state park page.',
  [W('Jarrell Plantation'),['Georgia State Parks: Jarrell Plantation',GSP+'JarrellPlantation']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Thursday to Sunday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day'),

N('high-falls-ga','High Falls ghost town ruins','High Falls State Park (Jackson)','ga','town','ruins',33.18123,-84.01602,'area','public','hike','','early 1800s–1880s',
  'In the early 1800s High Falls on the Towaliga River was a busy industrial town with stores, a grist mill, a cotton gin, a blacksmith shop, a shoe factory and a hotel. A major railroad passed it by in the 1880s and it became a ghost town. A bridge built on the Old Alabama Road in 1890 was mostly carried away by the flood of 1994.',
  'Trails along the river lead to the foundation of the grist mill and the ruins of a hydroelectric powerhouse; what is left of the 1890 bridge looks over the shoals below the dam. The falls themselves drop about 135 feet in all.',
  'A mill town the railroad skipped, now ruins by middle Georgia\'s biggest falls.',
  'High Falls State Park (Georgia Department of Natural Resources), 76 High Falls Park Drive. Parking fee. The historic trail is the yellow one on the park map; which lot it starts from was not checked. Hours from the state park page.',
  [W('High Falls State Park'),['Georgia State Parks: High Falls',GSP+'HighFalls']],
  brick='?',hours='Park 7 a.m. to 10 p.m.; office 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),

N('indian-springs-hotel','Indian Springs Hotel','Flovilla (Indian Springs)','ga','hotel','standing',33.24528,-83.92,'site','open','drive','hotels','1823 or 1825',
  'An inn at the mineral springs, built by the Creek leader William McIntosh and his cousin Joel Bailey. The Village at Indian Springs, which shows it, says it was built in 1823 and given a two-storey addition with a tavern, the Treaty Room, and a ballroom above in 1825; Wikipedia gives 1825 for the hotel. The Treaty of Indian Springs, by which the Creek ceded much of Georgia and Alabama, was signed here in 1825. It is now a museum.',
  'The Federal-style building with the Treaty Room tavern and the ballroom; the Village calls it the only antebellum mineral springs hotel still standing in Georgia. Interior not checked.',
  'The inn where the Creek signed away their Georgia land.',
  'Indian Spring Hotel Museum, at the Village at Indian Springs beside Indian Springs State Park. Open for tours on weekends from Memorial Day to Labor Day, by the Village\'s own page, which gives no times.',
  [W('Indian Springs Hotel Museum'),['Village at Indian Springs: hotel museum','https://thevillageatindiansprings.com/indian-spring-hotel-museum']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Weekends, Memorial Day to Labor Day (times not given)'),

N('cannonball-house-macon','Cannonball House','Macon','ga','landmark','standing',32.84002,-83.6322,'exact','open','drive','','1853',
  'A Greek Revival townhouse built in 1853 for Judge Asa Holt. On July 30, 1864, during the Battle of Dunlap Hill, a Union cannon under General George Stoneman fired from across the Ocmulgee River; the iron ball struck the sand sidewalk, bounced into a column of the front and went into the house. It is now a house museum run by the Friends of the Cannonball House.',
  'The house furnished as in 1853 to 1870, a Civil War museum, rooms of the first two sororities, founded at Wesleyan College, and behind it a hand-moulded two-storey brick kitchen with servants\' quarters upstairs. A bronze cannon cast at the Macon Arsenal in 1864 stands in the front yard.',
  'The house a Union cannonball hit, ball still on show.',
  'Cannonball House (nonprofit), 856 Mulberry Street. Admission charged. Open Tuesday to Saturday with tours every half hour, by the museum\'s own page, which gives no clock times.',
  [W('Cannonball House (Macon, Georgia)'),['Cannonball House','https://cannonballhouse.org/']],
  brick='?',hours='Tuesday to Saturday, tours every half hour'),

N('grand-opera-house-macon','Grand Opera House (The Grand)','Macon','ga','civic','standing',32.83884,-83.62821,'exact','open','drive','','1884',
  'Built in 1884 as the Academy of Music, with the largest stage in the southeastern United States and 2,418 seats; renovated in 1905 with the present seven-storey front and reopened as the Grand Opera House. Sarah Bernhardt, Harry Houdini, Will Rogers and the Allman Brothers Band played here. A group saved it from demolition in 1967 and it reopened as a live theatre in 1969; it is now Mercer University\'s performing arts center.',
  'The 1905 front and the theatre, about 1,030 seats now that the top balcony holds lights. One of Houdini\'s trap doors in the stage still works. Inside at a show.',
  'Houdini\'s trap door still works.',
  'Mercer University. Shows on the theatre\'s calendar; box office times not checked.',
  [W('Grand Opera House (Macon, Georgia)')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),

N('rome-clock-tower','Rome Clock Tower','Rome','ga','water','standing',34.25361,-85.16917,'exact','open','walk','','1871–1872',
  'Built in 1871 under James Noble Jr. as Rome\'s water tower: a tank of iron sheets from the Noble foundry, 63 feet deep and 26 feet across, held 250,000 gallons. A clock by the E. Howard company and a bronze bell dated 1872 were added on top the next year, making the tower 104 feet high. By the 1890s it could no longer meet the city\'s needs and stopped holding water.',
  'A ten-sided red brick tower round the tank on top of Clock Tower Hill, with a spiral stair of 107 steps between brick and tank, and four clock faces nine feet across. Tours climb to the top.',
  'A water tank wearing a clock tower.',
  'City of Rome landmark, East Second Street. Public tours are run in April, May, June, September and October by the Rome Area History Center, and group tours by appointment, by the Rome tourism office\'s page, which gives no times.',
  [W('Clock Tower (Rome, Georgia)'),['Georgia\'s Rome: Rome Clocktower','https://romegeorgia.org/city-clocktower/']],
  brick='yes',hours='Public tours April, May, June, September and October (times not given)'),

N('john-ross-house-rossville','John Ross House','Rossville','ga','landmark','standing',34.9811,-85.2848,'exact','view','drive','','1816–1817',
  'A two-storey dogtrot log house, home from 1830 to 1838 of John Ross, the long-serving leader of the Cherokee Nation who fought Removal. Legend says John McDonald, a Scot married to a Cherokee woman, built it in 1797; tree-ring dating points to 1816–17, probably by Ross himself. It was moved a short way from the middle of town in the 1960s and is a National Historic Landmark.',
  'Two log pens either side of an open breezeway under a low shingle roof, with a log-rail porch across the front; the chinking is modern cement. Interior not checked.',
  'The Cherokee chief\'s log home on the Trail of Tears.',
  'Owned by a local nonprofit (National Park Service), on a lane between Andrew Street and East Lake Avenue near downtown Rossville. Whether and when it opens was not checked; look from the lane.',
  [W('John Ross House (Rossville, Georgia)'),['NPS: John Ross House','https://www.nps.gov/places/john-ross-house-and-association-history-museum.htm']],
  brick='no'),

N('travelers-rest-toccoa','Traveler\'s Rest','Toccoa','ga','hotel','standing',34.60926,-83.23878,'exact','open','drive','hotels','about 1815–1825',
  'A stagecoach inn on the Unicoi Turnpike over the mountains, built by James R. Wyly: about 1815, the state says; between 1816 and 1825, says Wikipedia. Devereaux Jarrett bought it (in 1833 by the state, 1838 by Wikipedia), doubled it and made it the home of his 14,400-acre plantation worked by enslaved people; it also held the post office. Three generations of Jarretts lived here until the state bought the last few acres in 1955. It is a National Historic Landmark.',
  'The long wooden inn of wide pine planking with six chimneys, and inside many original Jarrett furnishings, among them a mantel with drawers for guests\' valuables; reconstructed outbuildings include slave quarters. Self-guided tours.',
  'Georgia\'s last stagecoach inn, the state says.',
  'Traveler\'s Rest State Historic Site (Georgia Department of Natural Resources), Riverdale Road off US 123, about six miles east of Toccoa. Adults $5.50, seniors $4.25, youth $3.50. Hours from the state park page.',
  [W('Travelers Rest (Toccoa, Georgia)'),['Georgia State Parks: Traveler\'s Rest',GSP+'TravelersRest']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Saturday and Sunday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day'),

N('clay-county-courthouse-ga','Clay County Courthouse','Fort Gaines','ga','civic','standing',31.6042,-85.04842,'exact','view','drive','','about 1871',
  'Built about 1871, a two-storey brick courthouse that the Register calls more like an antebellum plantation house than a courthouse, in a Carpenter style with classical details. It stands in the Fort Gaines Historic District, a 300-acre frontier town planned on the bluff over the Chattahoochee for military, political and trade purposes, with 327 contributing buildings.',
  'A hipped roof, a portico of square columns with a balcony on the second floor, and giant pilasters at each end of the front. Interior not checked.',
  'A courthouse that looks like a plantation house.',
  'Clay County (in use). Look from the street; the rest of the historic district is round it.',
  [W('Clay County Courthouse (Georgia)'),W('Fort Gaines Historic District')],
  brick='?'),

N('double-barreled-cannon-athens','Double-barreled cannon','Athens','ga','curio','standing',33.95997,-83.37639,'exact','public','walk','','1862',
  'In 1862 John Gilleland, an Athens dentist, builder and mechanic, raised $350 to cast a cannon with two side-by-side bores, splayed three degrees apart, to fire two balls joined by a chain that would mow down soldiers. At its trial the chain snapped: one ball knocked the chimney off a cabin and the other killed a cow. It never saw battle.',
  'The one-piece cannon on the front lawn of Athens City Hall, still pointing north. It is part of the Downtown Athens Historic District.',
  'The Confederate chain-shot cannon that killed a cow.',
  'City of Athens, City Hall lawn, downtown. Open ground.',
  [W('Double-barreled cannon')],
  brick='no'),
]

